        "orario": "15:20",
        "timestamp": 1775481654,
        "contenuto_esteso": "Advertisement US, Israel war on Iran China Diplomacy Pakistan’s peace plan a ‘critical opportunity’ for US-Iran talks ahead of Trump deadline Islamabad has put forward proposals for a ceasefire as the US president threatens ‘hell’ for Iran if the Strait of Hormuz does not reopen 3 -MIN READ 3 -MIN 10 Listen Shi Jiangtao Published: 9:20pm, 6 Apr 2026 Updated: 11:00pm, 6 Apr 2026 As US President Donald Trump’s Tuesday deadline for reopening the Strait of Hormuz approached, Pakistan put forward a fresh proposal for an immediate ceasefire on Monday, offering what one analyst described as “a critical opportunity” for talks. The plan was brokered through overnight contacts between Pakistani army chief Asim Munir, US officials including Vice-President J.D. Vance and Iran’s Foreign Minister Abbas Araghchi, according to Reuters. It called for an immediate halt to hostilities and the reopening of the vital waterway, followed by negotiations for a broader settlement within 15 to 20 days. Advertisement Both Washington and Tehran have not responded to the proposal. Pakistan’s latest mediation effort came as Trump threatened to rain down “hell” on Tehran if a deal was not reached by the end of Tuesday that would allow traffic to resume through the Strait of Hormuz, a key route for global energy supplies. Advertisement But Iran has ruled out reopening the strait immediately. One senior official told Reuters on Monday that Tehran would not accept deadlines while reviewing the proposal, arguing that Washington was not prepared to guarantee a permanent ceasefire. Advertisement Select Voice Select Speed 00:00 00:00 1.00"
